/* Original Geometry A lessons; append-only IDs preserve existing Biology progress. */
const GEOMETRY={"topics": [{"id": "geo-radicals", "title": "Radicals, without the panic", "desc": "A square root reverses squaring. √a means the principal, nonnegative square root. √25 = 5, while solving x² = 25 gives x = ±5.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 1", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 1, "parts": [["What does a root mean?", "A square root reverses squaring. √a means the principal, nonnegative square root. √25 = 5, while solving x² = 25 gives x = ±5."], ["Pull out a perfect square", "To simplify √72, find a perfect-square factor: √(36 × 2) = 6√2. Keep factoring until no perfect square greater than 1 remains inside."], ["Variables need a small safety check", "√(x²) = |x|, not always x. For example, if x = −3, the root is 3. If a problem states x ≥ 0, then √(x²) = x."]], "trap": "√(a + b) is not generally √a + √b. A radical symbol alone gives the nonnegative root.", "example": "An exact diagonal length such as 6√2 keeps all the precision; 8.49 is only a rounded estimate.", "model": "geo-radical"}, {"id": "geo-radical-operations", "title": "Add, multiply & rationalize", "desc": "Combine coefficients only when the simplified radicands match. √8 + √18 = 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 1", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 1, "parts": [["Only matching roots combine", "Combine coefficients only when the simplified radicands match. √8 + √18 = 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2."], ["Multiply every needed pair", "Multiply coefficients and radicands, then simplify. Use distribution: √2(3 + √2) = 3√2 + 2. For two binomials, multiply all four pairs."], ["Clear a root from the bottom", "Rationalize a monomial denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by a suitable radical. For a binomial use its conjugate: (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "Conjugates are partners with the same terms and the opposite middle sign. Their product removes the radical cross terms.", "model": "geo-radical"}, {"id": "geo-right-triangles", "title": "Right triangles & exact distance", "desc": "The Pythagorean theorem applies to right triangles: a² + b² = c². The hypotenuse c is opposite the right angle; a and b are the legs.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 1", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 1, "parts": [["Find the side across from the square corner", "The Pythagorean theorem applies to right triangles: a² + b² = c². The hypotenuse c is opposite the right angle; a and b are the legs."], ["Add to find the long side; subtract for a leg", "With legs 6 and 8, c = √(36 + 64) = 10. With hypotenuse 10 and one leg 6, the other is √(100 − 36) = 8. Lengths are positive."], ["Coordinates hide a right triangle", "Horizontal and vertical changes form the legs of a right triangle. Distance = √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²). Keep an exact radical unless rounding is requested."]], "trap": "Never use a² + b² = c² on a triangle unless it is a right triangle. c must be the hypotenuse.", "example": "A square tile with side 5 has diagonal 5√2, not 10: the diagonal cuts a right triangle across the tile.", "model": "geo-triangle"}, {"id": "geo-factoring", "title": "Quadratics: factor & solve", "desc": "Write the quadratic in standard form ax² + bx + c = 0, with a ≠ 0. Check for a greatest common factor before factoring further.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 2", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 2, "parts": [["Give the equation a zero side", "Write the quadratic in standard form ax² + bx + c = 0, with a ≠ 0. Check for a greatest common factor before factoring further."], ["A zero product gives choices", "The zero-product property says AB = 0 implies A = 0 or B = 0. So x² + x − 6 = (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0 has solutions −3 and 2."], ["Check the original equation", "Substitute each candidate solution into the original equation. Do not lose a solution by dividing by x: x(x − 4) = 0 has both x = 0 and x = 4."]], "trap": "You cannot set each factor equal to zero until the product itself equals zero.", "example": "A rectangle area equation can become a quadratic. Keep only solutions that give positive physical dimensions.", "model": "geo-quadratic"}, {"id": "geo-formula", "title": "The quadratic formula", "desc": "First write ax² + bx + c = 0. The quadratic formula is x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a). Use parentheses when substituting negatives.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 2", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 2, "parts": [["Read a, b, and c with their signs", "First write ax² + bx + c = 0. The quadratic formula is x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a). Use parentheses when substituting negatives."], ["The plus-or-minus makes two paths", "For x² − 2x − 3 = 0, x = (2 ± √16)/2 = (2 ± 4)/2, giving 3 and −1. The entire numerator is divided by 2a."], ["The number under the root is a clue", "The discriminant D = b² − 4ac determines real-root count. D > 0 gives two distinct real roots; D = 0 gives one repeated real root; D < 0 gives no real roots."]], "trap": "The denominator is 2a, not just 2. Squaring a negative b requires parentheses.", "example": "The graph crosses the x-axis twice, touches it once, or misses it according to the discriminant.", "model": "geo-quadratic"}, {"id": "geo-complete-square", "title": "Completing the square", "desc": "To complete the square, divide by the leading coefficient if needed, then move the constant. Start with x² + bx = k.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 2", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 2, "parts": [["Make the x² coefficient one", "To complete the square, divide by the leading coefficient if needed, then move the constant. Start with x² + bx = k."], ["Half, then square, then both sides", "Add (b/2)² to both sides of x² + bx = k. Then x² + bx + (b/2)² = (x + b/2)². Keeping both sides balanced preserves equality."], ["Finish with both square-root choices", "After forming a square, use ± when taking square roots to solve the equation. (x + 3)² = 16 gives x = −3 ± 4, or 1 and −7."]], "trap": "Adding to just one side changes the equation. Taking a square root without ± can lose a solution.", "example": "Completing a square turns an awkward quadratic into one squared expression you already know how to undo.", "model": "geo-quadratic"}, {"id": "geo-systems", "title": "Systems: where equations agree", "desc": "A solution to a two-variable system is an ordered pair (x,y) satisfying every equation. Graphically it is an intersection point.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 2", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 2, "parts": [["One answer must fit both rules", "A solution to a two-variable system is an ordered pair (x,y) satisfying every equation. Graphically it is an intersection point."], ["Substitute or eliminate", "Substitution replaces a variable with an equal expression. Elimination multiplies equations as needed and adds or subtracts them to cancel a variable. Solve, then find the other coordinate."], ["Translate a rectangle story", "For area 24 and perimeter 20: LW = 24 and L + W = 10. Substitute W = 10 − L to get L(10 − L) = 24. The dimensions are 4 and 6."]], "trap": "An x-value alone is not a full ordered-pair solution. Check both coordinates in both original equations.", "example": "For y = x² and y = x + 2, intersections satisfy x² = x + 2, giving (−1,1) and (2,4).", "model": "geo-system"}, {"id": "geo-points", "title": "Points, segments & rays", "desc": "A point has no dimensions. A line extends infinitely in both directions. A segment has two endpoints. A ray has one endpoint and extends infinitely in one direction.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 3", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 3, "parts": [["Know what stops and what keeps going", "A point has no dimensions. A line extends infinitely in both directions. A segment has two endpoints. A ray has one endpoint and extends infinitely in one direction."], ["Read coordinates in order", "In (x,y), x gives horizontal position and y vertical position. Quadrants I, II, III, IV have signs (+,+), (−,+), (−,−), (+,−). Points on axes are not in quadrants."], ["Pieces make the whole", "Segment addition: if B lies between A and C, AB + BC = AC. For AB = 2x, BC = x + 3, AC = 18, solve 3x + 3 = 18, so x = 5."]], "trap": "Ray AB and ray BA generally point in different directions. Do not add pieces unless the diagram or text establishes their positions.", "example": "A trip from A to C through a point B on the segment has two lengths that add to the full distance.", "model": "geo-segment"}, {"id": "geo-midpoints", "title": "Midpoints & segment bisectors", "desc": "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 3", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 3, "parts": [["A midpoint makes equal halves", "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint."], ["Average x, then average y", "M = ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2). For a missing endpoint, double the midpoint coordinates and subtract the known endpoint coordinates."], ["Construct the middle with equal arcs", "Use the same compass radius, greater than half the segment length, from both endpoints. Join the two arc intersections. This line crosses the segment at its midpoint and at 90°."]], "trap": "A segment bisector alone guarantees equal halves, not a 90° angle.", "example": "If one half is 3x + 1 and the other is x + 9, set them equal: x = 4, so each half is 13.", "model": "geo-midpoint"}, {"id": "geo-slope", "title": "Slope: rise over run", "desc": "m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), when x₂ ≠ x₁. Keep endpoint order consistent. Positive slopes rise left to right; negative slopes fall.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 3", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 3, "parts": [["Slope measures steepness and direction", "m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), when x₂ ≠ x₁. Keep endpoint order consistent. Positive slopes rise left to right; negative slopes fall."], ["Horizontal is zero; vertical is undefined", "A horizontal line has equation y = k and slope 0. A vertical line has equation x = k and undefined slope. Remember HOY / VUX."], ["Check whether points share a line", "For distinct points, equal slopes from a shared point show collinearity, provided the slopes exist. If all x-coordinates match, the points lie on one vertical line."]], "trap": "Zero slope and undefined slope are different. A vertical line is x = a number, not y = a number.", "example": "A ramp rising 2 units across a horizontal run of 5 has slope 2/5.", "model": "geo-slope"}, {"id": "geo-line-equations", "title": "Build a line equation", "desc": "In y = mx + b, m is slope and (0,b) is the y-intercept. This form describes nonvertical lines. A vertical line is x = k.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 3", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 3, "parts": [["Use y = mx + b", "In y = mx + b, m is slope and (0,b) is the y-intercept. This form describes nonvertical lines. A vertical line is x = k."], ["Use a point to find b", "Find m, substitute a given point into y = mx + b, solve for b, then write the equation using variables x and y. With m = 3 through (2,7), y = 3x + 1."], ["Check the finished rule", "For y = 3x + 1, the point (2,7) works because 7 = 3(2) + 1. With two given points, check both; also check that the slope matches."]], "trap": "The b is a y-intercept, not whichever y-value appears in the given point.", "example": "Through (1,4) and (3,8), slope is 2 and b is 2, giving y = 2x + 2.", "model": "geo-slope"}, {"id": "geo-angles", "title": "Angles & equal halves", "desc": "An angle is formed by two rays sharing a vertex. The vertex is the middle letter in a three-letter name. Acute: 0° < θ < 90°; right: 90°; obtuse: 90° < θ < 180°; straight: 180°.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 4", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 4, "parts": [["Name and measure an angle", "An angle is formed by two rays sharing a vertex. The vertex is the middle letter in a three-letter name. Acute: 0° < θ < 90°; right: 90°; obtuse: 90° < θ < 180°; straight: 180°."], ["90, 180, or 360?", "Complementary angles sum to 90°; supplementary angles sum to 180°. A linear pair forms a straight angle, so its angles sum to 180°. Angles around one point sum to 360°."], ["Bisect means two equal angles", "An angle bisector divides an angle into two congruent angles. For halves 3x + 5 and 5x − 15, equate them: x = 10, so each half is 35° and the whole is 70°."], ["Copy an angle with a compass", "Draw a new base ray. From the original vertex, draw an arc crossing both angle rays. With the same radius, draw an arc from the new vertex. Transfer the distance between the original two arc intersections onto the new arc, then join the new vertex to that mark."]], "trap": "A picture that looks like equal angles is not proof. Use markings, a stated bisector, or a known relationship.", "example": "For adjacent angles 28° and 47°, the whole is 75°. Its bisector would make two 37.5° angles.", "model": "geo-angle"}, {"id": "geo-parallel", "title": "Parallel lines & angle pairs", "desc": "For two distinct coplanar parallel lines cut by a transversal, corresponding, alternate interior, and alternate exterior angles are congruent. Same-side interior angles are supplementary.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 4", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 4, "parts": [["A transversal crosses two lines", "For two distinct coplanar parallel lines cut by a transversal, corresponding, alternate interior, and alternate exterior angles are congruent. Same-side interior angles are supplementary."], ["Spot matching or adding pairs", "Corresponding angles occupy the same relative position at each intersection. Alternate interior pairs are between the lines on opposite sides of the transversal; alternate exterior pairs are outside on opposite sides."], ["Turn the relationship into an equation", "For parallel lines, corresponding angles 2x + 10 and 70 yield x = 30. Same-side interior angles 2x and 3x yield 5x = 180, so x = 36."]], "trap": "The special equal-angle rules require parallel lines. A transversal alone is not enough.", "example": "If one angle in the parallel-line model is 65°, all the acute angles are 65° and all the obtuse angles are 115°.", "model": "geo-parallel"}, {"id": "geo-perpendicular", "title": "Parallel & perpendicular equations", "desc": "Distinct nonvertical parallel lines have equal slopes and different intercepts. Distinct vertical lines are parallel to each other.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 4", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 4, "parts": [["Parallel lines share direction", "Distinct nonvertical parallel lines have equal slopes and different intercepts. Distinct vertical lines are parallel to each other."], ["Perpendicular lines make 90°", "Nonvertical perpendicular lines have slopes whose product is −1. Use the negative reciprocal. Horizontal and vertical lines are perpendicular to each other."], ["Find the new line through a point", "A line perpendicular to y = 2x + 1 through (0,3) has slope −1/2 and intercept 3: y = −x/2 + 3. For a vertical answer, use x = k."]], "trap": "Changing only the sign is not enough: the perpendicular slope to 2 is −1/2, not −2.", "example": "A line parallel to y = 3x − 4 through (2,1) is y = 3x − 5.", "model": "geo-lines"}, {"id": "geo-centers", "title": "Bisectors, incenters & circumcenters", "desc": "A perpendicular bisector passes through a segment’s midpoint at 90°. Every point on it is equidistant from the endpoints. To find its equation, combine the midpoint with a perpendicular slope.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 4", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 4, "parts": [["A perpendicular bisector does two jobs", "A perpendicular bisector passes through a segment’s midpoint at 90°. Every point on it is equidistant from the endpoints. To find its equation, combine the midpoint with a perpendicular slope."], ["Incenter: equal distance to sides", "The incenter is the common intersection of the triangle’s three internal angle bisectors. It is equidistant from the sides and is the center of the inscribed circle. Distance to a side is measured perpendicularly."], ["Circumcenter: equal distance to corners", "The circumcenter is the intersection of the side perpendicular bisectors. It is equidistant from the three vertices and is the center of the circumscribed circle. It can lie outside the triangle."]], "trap": "Incenter uses angle bisectors and distances to sides. Circumcenter uses perpendicular bisectors and distances to vertices.", "example": "For a segment from (0,0) to (4,0), the perpendicular bisector is x = 2; every point on it is equally far from both endpoints.", "model": "geo-centers"}, {"id": "geo-angle-strategy", "title": "Unknown angles: build a plan", "desc": "Identify the smallest useful relationship: linear pair, vertical angles, triangle sum, full turn, bisector, or parallel-line angle pair. Mark known values before solving.", "icon": "◇", "group": "Unit 4", "subject": "geometry", "unit": 4, "parts": [["Start with one certain relationship", "Identify the smallest useful relationship: linear pair, vertical angles, triangle sum, full turn, bisector, or parallel-line angle pair. Mark known values before solving."], ["Name the unknown and solve", "For triangle angles x, 2x, and 3x, write x + 2x + 3x = 180. Then x = 30°, giving angles 30°, 60°, and 90°."], ["Add a helpful line when it unlocks a clue", "An auxiliary line is a line you add to a diagram to make useful relationships visible. A parallel through a vertex can reveal alternate interior or corresponding angles. Explain the reason for adding it."]], "trap": "If the problem asks for an angle written 3x + 5, finding x is only the first step. Substitute to finish.", "example": "If a triangle’s angles are x + 10, 2x, and 80°, then 3x + 90 = 180, so x = 30° and the angles are 40°, 60°, 80°.", "model": "geo-angle"}], "questions": [{"topic": "geo-radicals", "prompt": "Simplify √50.", "options": ["25√2", "5√2", "10√5", "5"], "answer": 1, "why": "50 = 25 × 2, so √50 = 5√2."}, {"topic": "geo-radicals", "prompt": "Which number is irrational?", "options": ["√49", "0.75", "√3", "−4"], "answer": 2, "why": "3 is not a perfect square. √3 cannot be written as a ratio of integers."}, {"topic": "geo-radicals", "prompt": "For any real x, √(x²) equals…", "options": ["x", "−x", "x²", "|x|"], "answer": 3, "why": "The principal square root is nonnegative, so it equals the absolute value of x."}, {"topic": "geo-radical-operations", "prompt": "Simplify √12 + √27.", "options": ["√39", "5√3", "13√3", "3√5"], "answer": 1, "why": "√12 = 2√3 and √27 = 3√3, so the sum is 5√3."}, {"topic": "geo-radical-operations", "prompt": "Simplify (√5 + 2)(√5 − 2).", "options": ["1", "9", "√21", "−1"], "answer": 0, "why": "This is a difference of squares: 5 − 4 = 1."}, {"topic": "geo-radical-operations", "prompt": "Rationalize 3/√3.", "options": ["3√3", "1/3", "√3", "√3/3"], "answer": 2, "why": "Multiply top and bottom by √3: 3√3/3 = √3."}, {"topic": "geo-right-triangles", "prompt": "A right triangle has legs 5 and 12. Its hypotenuse is…", "options": ["7", "13", "17", "√119"], "answer": 1, "why": "c = √(25 + 144) = √169 = 13."}, {"topic": "geo-right-triangles", "prompt": "Hypotenuse 13, one leg 5. Find the other leg.", "options": ["18", "√194", "8", "12"], "answer": 3, "why": "The missing leg is √(13² − 5²) = √144 = 12."}, {"topic": "geo-right-triangles", "prompt": "Distance from (−1,1) to (2,5)?", "options": ["5", "7", "√7", "25"], "answer": 0, "why": "The changes are 3 and 4, so distance = √(9 + 16) = 5."}, {"topic": "geo-factoring", "prompt": "Solve x² − 9 = 0.", "options": ["x = 9 only", "x = 3 only", "x = −3 or 3", "x = −9 or 9"], "answer": 2, "why": "(x − 3)(x + 3) = 0 gives x = 3 or −3."}, {"topic": "geo-factoring", "prompt": "Solve x(x − 5) = 0.", "options": ["0 and 5", "5 only", "0 and −5", "−5 only"], "answer": 0, "why": "Either x = 0 or x − 5 = 0. Dividing by x would lose the zero solution."}, {"topic": "geo-factoring", "prompt": "Before using the zero-product property, you need…", "options": ["A positive leading coefficient", "A product equal to zero", "A linear equation", "A decimal answer"], "answer": 1, "why": "The property requires a product equal to zero."}, {"topic": "geo-formula", "prompt": "For 3x² − 7x + 2 = 0, b is…", "options": ["7", "−7", "2", "3"], "answer": 1, "why": "The coefficient of x is −7, including its sign."}, {"topic": "geo-formula", "prompt": "A discriminant of −12 means…", "options": ["Two real roots", "One repeated real root", "No real roots", "Twelve real roots"], "answer": 2, "why": "A negative number has no real square root, so the quadratic has no real solutions."}, {"topic": "geo-formula", "prompt": "Solve x² − 2x − 3 = 0.", "options": ["−3 and 1", "3 and −1", "2 and 3", "−2 and −3"], "answer": 1, "why": "The formula gives (2 ± 4)/2, so x = 3 or −1."}, {"topic": "geo-complete-square", "prompt": "What completes x² + 10x + ___?", "options": ["5", "10", "20", "25"], "answer": 3, "why": "Half of 10 is 5, and 5² = 25."}, {"topic": "geo-complete-square", "prompt": "Solve (x + 2)² = 9.", "options": ["1 and −5", "1 only", "5 and −1", "−5 only"], "answer": 0, "why": "x + 2 = ±3, so x = 1 or −5."}, {"topic": "geo-complete-square", "prompt": "To complete x² + 4x = 12, add…", "options": ["4 to the left only", "2 to both sides", "4 to both sides", "16 to both sides"], "answer": 2, "why": "Half of 4 is 2; its square is 4. Add 4 to both sides."}, {"topic": "geo-systems", "prompt": "Solve y = x + 1 and x + y = 7.", "options": ["(4,3)", "(3,4)", "(2,5)", "(7,8)"], "answer": 1, "why": "Substitute: x + x + 1 = 7, so x = 3 and y = 4."}, {"topic": "geo-systems", "prompt": "y = x² and y = 4 have which intersections?", "options": ["(2,4) only", "(−2,4) only", "(−2,4) and (2,4)", "(4,2) and (4,−2)"], "answer": 2, "why": "x² = 4 gives x = ±2; both have y = 4."}, {"topic": "geo-systems", "prompt": "Area 24, perimeter 20: rectangle side lengths?", "options": ["2 and 12", "3 and 8", "4 and 6", "5 and 5"], "answer": 2, "why": "4 × 6 = 24 and 2(4 + 6) = 20."}, {"topic": "geo-points", "prompt": "Which object has exactly one endpoint?", "options": ["Line", "Point", "Ray", "Segment"], "answer": 2, "why": "A ray begins at one endpoint and extends without end in one direction."}, {"topic": "geo-points", "prompt": "Which quadrant contains (−4,2)?", "options": ["I", "II", "III", "IV"], "answer": 1, "why": "Negative x and positive y put the point in quadrant II."}, {"topic": "geo-points", "prompt": "B is between A and C. AB = 7, AC = 19. BC = ?", "options": ["26", "12", "7", "19"], "answer": 1, "why": "AB + BC = AC, so BC = 19 − 7 = 12."}, {"topic": "geo-midpoints", "prompt": "Midpoint of (−2,4) and (6,0)?", "options": ["(4,4)", "(2,2)", "(2,4)", "(−4,2)"], "answer": 1, "why": "Average x: (−2 + 6)/2 = 2; average y: (4 + 0)/2 = 2."}, {"topic": "geo-midpoints", "prompt": "M(3,5) is midpoint; A(1,2). Find B.", "options": ["(2,3)", "(4,7)", "(5,8)", "(6,10)"], "answer": 2, "why": "B = (2·3 − 1, 2·5 − 2) = (5,8)."}, {"topic": "geo-midpoints", "prompt": "Every segment bisector must…", "options": ["Be vertical", "Make a right angle", "Pass through the midpoint", "Be a full line"], "answer": 2, "why": "A bisector passes through the midpoint; perpendicularity is an extra condition."}, {"topic": "geo-slope", "prompt": "Slope through (1,2) and (3,8)?", "options": ["1/3", "3", "6", "−3"], "answer": 1, "why": "m = (8 − 2)/(3 − 1) = 6/2 = 3."}, {"topic": "geo-slope", "prompt": "What is the slope of x = −2?", "options": ["−2", "0", "Undefined", "2"], "answer": 2, "why": "x = −2 is vertical, so its horizontal change is zero."}, {"topic": "geo-slope", "prompt": "A horizontal line through (4,−3) is…", "options": ["x = 4", "y = 4", "x = −3", "y = −3"], "answer": 3, "why": "All points on the horizontal line have the same y-coordinate, −3."}, {"topic": "geo-line-equations", "prompt": "Slope 2 through (3,7). Equation?", "options": ["y = 2x + 7", "y = 2x + 1", "y = 3x + 2", "x = 3"], "answer": 1, "why": "7 = 2(3) + b gives b = 1."}, {"topic": "geo-line-equations", "prompt": "Vertical line through (5,−2)?", "options": ["y = −2", "y = 5", "x = 5", "x = −2"], "answer": 2, "why": "A vertical line keeps x fixed, so x = 5."}, {"topic": "geo-line-equations", "prompt": "y = −3x + 4 has y-intercept…", "options": ["(0,4)", "(4,0)", "(0,−3)", "(−3,0)"], "answer": 0, "why": "Set x = 0: y = 4."}, {"topic": "geo-angles", "prompt": "In ∠PQR, the vertex is…", "options": ["P", "Q", "R", "Any letter"], "answer": 1, "why": "The middle letter Q names the shared endpoint."}, {"topic": "geo-angles", "prompt": "An angle complements 34°. Its measure is…", "options": ["146°", "56°", "34°", "66°"], "answer": 1, "why": "Complementary angles sum to 90°: 90 − 34 = 56."}, {"topic": "geo-angles", "prompt": "A bisector divides a 126° angle into…", "options": ["Two 63° angles", "Two 126° angles", "36° and 90°", "Two 62° angles"], "answer": 0, "why": "Bisect means two congruent halves, and 126/2 = 63."}, {"topic": "geo-parallel", "prompt": "Parallel lines: an alternate interior partner of 68° measures…", "options": ["112°", "22°", "68°", "136°"], "answer": 2, "why": "Alternate interior angles are congruent when the lines are parallel."}, {"topic": "geo-parallel", "prompt": "Parallel lines: same-side interior angles are 3x and 2x. x = ?", "options": ["18", "36", "45", "90"], "answer": 1, "why": "3x + 2x = 180 gives x = 36."}, {"topic": "geo-parallel", "prompt": "Before using corresponding-angle congruence here, confirm…", "options": ["The lines are parallel", "The angles look equal", "The transversal is horizontal", "All angles are acute"], "answer": 0, "why": "Parallel lines guarantee corresponding angles are congruent."}, {"topic": "geo-perpendicular", "prompt": "Perpendicular slope to 3/4?", "options": ["−3/4", "4/3", "−4/3", "3/4"], "answer": 2, "why": "The negative reciprocal is −4/3; their product is −1."}, {"topic": "geo-perpendicular", "prompt": "Parallel to y = −2x + 5 through (1,1)?", "options": ["y = 2x − 1", "y = −2x + 3", "y = x + 1", "x = 1"], "answer": 1, "why": "Use slope −2. Then 1 = −2(1) + b, so b = 3."}, {"topic": "geo-perpendicular", "prompt": "A line perpendicular to a horizontal line is…", "options": ["Horizontal", "Vertical", "Always slope 1", "Always slope −1"], "answer": 1, "why": "A vertical line meets a horizontal line at 90°."}, {"topic": "geo-centers", "prompt": "Which point is equidistant from all three triangle sides?", "options": ["Circumcenter", "Incenter", "Any vertex", "Any midpoint"], "answer": 1, "why": "The incenter lies on the angle bisectors and is equidistant from the sides."}, {"topic": "geo-centers", "prompt": "A circumcircle passes through…", "options": ["All three vertices", "All three side midpoints", "Only one vertex", "The incenter only"], "answer": 0, "why": "The circumcenter is equidistant from the vertices, so its circle passes through all three."}, {"topic": "geo-centers", "prompt": "Perpendicular bisector of (0,0) to (6,0)?", "options": ["y = 3", "x = 6", "y = 0", "x = 3"], "answer": 3, "why": "The midpoint is (3,0); the segment is horizontal, so its perpendicular bisector is vertical, x = 3."}, {"topic": "geo-angle-strategy", "prompt": "Triangle angles 45°, 65°, and θ. θ = ?", "options": ["70°", "80°", "110°", "250°"], "answer": 0, "why": "Triangle angles sum to 180°: 180 − 45 − 65 = 70."}, {"topic": "geo-angle-strategy", "prompt": "Angles around a point total…", "options": ["90°", "180°", "270°", "360°"], "answer": 3, "why": "One full turn is 360°."}, {"topic": "geo-angle-strategy", "prompt": "x = 20 and an angle is 3x + 5 degrees. The angle is…", "options": ["20°", "60°", "65°", "25°"], "answer": 2, "why": "Substitute x = 20: 3(20) + 5 = 65°."}], "cards": [{"topic": "geo-radicals", "section": 0, "front": "What does a root mean? — explain it in your own words.", "back": "A square root reverses squaring. √a means the principal, nonnegative square root. √25 = 5, while solving x² = 25 gives x = ±5."}, {"topic": "geo-radicals", "section": 1, "front": "Pull out a perfect square — explain it in your own words.", "back": "To simplify √72, find a perfect-square factor: √(36 × 2) = 6√2. Keep factoring until no perfect square greater than 1 remains inside."}, {"topic": "geo-radicals", "section": 2, "front": "Variables need a small safety check — explain it in your own words.", "back": "√(x²) = |x|, not always x. For example, if x = −3, the root is 3. If a problem states x ≥ 0, then √(x²) = x."}, {"topic": "geo-radical-operations", "section": 0, "front": "Only matching roots combine — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Combine coefficients only when the simplified radicands match. √8 + √18 = 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2."}, {"topic": "geo-radical-operations", "section": 1, "front": "Multiply every needed pair — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Multiply coefficients and radicands, then simplify. Use distribution: √2(3 + √2) = 3√2 + 2. For two binomials, multiply all four pairs."}, {"topic": "geo-radical-operations", "section": 2, "front": "Clear a root from the bottom — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Rationalize a monomial denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by a suitable radical. For a binomial use its conjugate: (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b."}, {"topic": "geo-right-triangles", "section": 0, "front": "Find the side across from the square corner — explain it in your own words.", "back": "The Pythagorean theorem applies to right triangles: a² + b² = c². The hypotenuse c is opposite the right angle; a and b are the legs."}, {"topic": "geo-right-triangles", "section": 1, "front": "Add to find the long side; subtract for a leg — explain it in your own words.", "back": "With legs 6 and 8, c = √(36 + 64) = 10. With hypotenuse 10 and one leg 6, the other is √(100 − 36) = 8. Lengths are positive."}, {"topic": "geo-right-triangles", "section": 2, "front": "Coordinates hide a right triangle — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Horizontal and vertical changes form the legs of a right triangle. Distance = √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²). Keep an exact radical unless rounding is requested."}, {"topic": "geo-factoring", "section": 0, "front": "Give the equation a zero side — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Write the quadratic in standard form ax² + bx + c = 0, with a ≠ 0. Check for a greatest common factor before factoring further."}, {"topic": "geo-factoring", "section": 1, "front": "A zero product gives choices — explain it in your own words.", "back": "The zero-product property says AB = 0 implies A = 0 or B = 0. So x² + x − 6 = (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0 has solutions −3 and 2."}, {"topic": "geo-factoring", "section": 2, "front": "Check the original equation — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Substitute each candidate solution into the original equation. Do not lose a solution by dividing by x: x(x − 4) = 0 has both x = 0 and x = 4."}, {"topic": "geo-formula", "section": 0, "front": "Read a, b, and c with their signs — explain it in your own words.", "back": "First write ax² + bx + c = 0. The quadratic formula is x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a). Use parentheses when substituting negatives."}, {"topic": "geo-formula", "section": 1, "front": "The plus-or-minus makes two paths — explain it in your own words.", "back": "For x² − 2x − 3 = 0, x = (2 ± √16)/2 = (2 ± 4)/2, giving 3 and −1. The entire numerator is divided by 2a."}, {"topic": "geo-formula", "section": 2, "front": "The number under the root is a clue — explain it in your own words.", "back": "The discriminant D = b² − 4ac determines real-root count. D > 0 gives two distinct real roots; D = 0 gives one repeated real root; D < 0 gives no real roots."}, {"topic": "geo-complete-square", "section": 0, "front": "Make the x² coefficient one — explain it in your own words.", "back": "To complete the square, divide by the leading coefficient if needed, then move the constant. Start with x² + bx = k."}, {"topic": "geo-complete-square", "section": 1, "front": "Half, then square, then both sides — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Add (b/2)² to both sides of x² + bx = k. Then x² + bx + (b/2)² = (x + b/2)². Keeping both sides balanced preserves equality."}, {"topic": "geo-complete-square", "section": 2, "front": "Finish with both square-root choices — explain it in your own words.", "back": "After forming a square, use ± when taking square roots to solve the equation. (x + 3)² = 16 gives x = −3 ± 4, or 1 and −7."}, {"topic": "geo-systems", "section": 0, "front": "One answer must fit both rules — explain it in your own words.", "back": "A solution to a two-variable system is an ordered pair (x,y) satisfying every equation. Graphically it is an intersection point."}, {"topic": "geo-systems", "section": 1, "front": "Substitute or eliminate — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Substitution replaces a variable with an equal expression. Elimination multiplies equations as needed and adds or subtracts them to cancel a variable. Solve, then find the other coordinate."}, {"topic": "geo-systems", "section": 2, "front": "Translate a rectangle story — explain it in your own words.", "back": "For area 24 and perimeter 20: LW = 24 and L + W = 10. Substitute W = 10 − L to get L(10 − L) = 24. The dimensions are 4 and 6."}, {"topic": "geo-points", "section": 0, "front": "Know what stops and what keeps going — explain it in your own words.", "back": "A point has no dimensions. A line extends infinitely in both directions. A segment has two endpoints. A ray has one endpoint and extends infinitely in one direction."}, {"topic": "geo-points", "section": 1, "front": "Read coordinates in order — explain it in your own words.", "back": "In (x,y), x gives horizontal position and y vertical position. Quadrants I, II, III, IV have signs (+,+), (−,+), (−,−), (+,−). Points on axes are not in quadrants."}, {"topic": "geo-points", "section": 2, "front": "Pieces make the whole — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Segment addition: if B lies between A and C, AB + BC = AC. For AB = 2x, BC = x + 3, AC = 18, solve 3x + 3 = 18, so x = 5."}, {"topic": "geo-midpoints", "section": 0, "front": "A midpoint makes equal halves — explain it in your own words.", "back": "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint."}, {"topic": "geo-midpoints", "section": 1, "front": "Average x, then average y — explain it in your own words.", "back": "M = ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2). For a missing endpoint, double the midpoint coordinates and subtract the known endpoint coordinates."}, {"topic": "geo-midpoints", "section": 2, "front": "Construct the middle with equal arcs — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Use the same compass radius, greater than half the segment length, from both endpoints. Join the two arc intersections. This line crosses the segment at its midpoint and at 90°."}, {"topic": "geo-slope", "section": 0, "front": "Slope measures steepness and direction — explain it in your own words.", "back": "m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), when x₂ ≠ x₁. Keep endpoint order consistent. Positive slopes rise left to right; negative slopes fall."}, {"topic": "geo-slope", "section": 1, "front": "Horizontal is zero; vertical is undefined — explain it in your own words.", "back": "A horizontal line has equation y = k and slope 0. A vertical line has equation x = k and undefined slope. Remember HOY / VUX."}, {"topic": "geo-slope", "section": 2, "front": "Check whether points share a line — explain it in your own words.", "back": "For distinct points, equal slopes from a shared point show collinearity, provided the slopes exist. If all x-coordinates match, the points lie on one vertical line."}, {"topic": "geo-line-equations", "section": 0, "front": "Use y = mx + b — explain it in your own words.", "back": "In y = mx + b, m is slope and (0,b) is the y-intercept. This form describes nonvertical lines. A vertical line is x = k."}, {"topic": "geo-line-equations", "section": 1, "front": "Use a point to find b — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Find m, substitute a given point into y = mx + b, solve for b, then write the equation using variables x and y. With m = 3 through (2,7), y = 3x + 1."}, {"topic": "geo-line-equations", "section": 2, "front": "Check the finished rule — explain it in your own words.", "back": "For y = 3x + 1, the point (2,7) works because 7 = 3(2) + 1. With two given points, check both; also check that the slope matches."}, {"topic": "geo-angles", "section": 0, "front": "Name and measure an angle — explain it in your own words.", "back": "An angle is formed by two rays sharing a vertex. The vertex is the middle letter in a three-letter name. Acute: 0° < θ < 90°; right: 90°; obtuse: 90° < θ < 180°; straight: 180°."}, {"topic": "geo-angles", "section": 1, "front": "90, 180, or 360? — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Complementary angles sum to 90°; supplementary angles sum to 180°. A linear pair forms a straight angle, so its angles sum to 180°. Angles around one point sum to 360°."}, {"topic": "geo-angles", "section": 2, "front": "Bisect means two equal angles — explain it in your own words.", "back": "An angle bisector divides an angle into two congruent angles. For halves 3x + 5 and 5x − 15, equate them: x = 10, so each half is 35° and the whole is 70°."}, {"topic": "geo-parallel", "section": 0, "front": "A transversal crosses two lines — explain it in your own words.", "back": "For two distinct coplanar parallel lines cut by a transversal, corresponding, alternate interior, and alternate exterior angles are congruent. Same-side interior angles are supplementary."}, {"topic": "geo-parallel", "section": 1, "front": "Spot matching or adding pairs — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Corresponding angles occupy the same relative position at each intersection. Alternate interior pairs are between the lines on opposite sides of the transversal; alternate exterior pairs are outside on opposite sides."}, {"topic": "geo-parallel", "section": 2, "front": "Turn the relationship into an equation — explain it in your own words.", "back": "For parallel lines, corresponding angles 2x + 10 and 70 yield x = 30. Same-side interior angles 2x and 3x yield 5x = 180, so x = 36."}, {"topic": "geo-perpendicular", "section": 0, "front": "Parallel lines share direction — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Distinct nonvertical parallel lines have equal slopes and different intercepts. Distinct vertical lines are parallel to each other."}, {"topic": "geo-perpendicular", "section": 1, "front": "Perpendicular lines make 90° — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Nonvertical perpendicular lines have slopes whose product is −1. Use the negative reciprocal. Horizontal and vertical lines are perpendicular to each other."}, {"topic": "geo-perpendicular", "section": 2, "front": "Find the new line through a point — explain it in your own words.", "back": "A line perpendicular to y = 2x + 1 through (0,3) has slope −1/2 and intercept 3: y = −x/2 + 3. For a vertical answer, use x = k."}, {"topic": "geo-centers", "section": 0, "front": "A perpendicular bisector does two jobs — explain it in your own words.", "back": "A perpendicular bisector passes through a segment’s midpoint at 90°. Every point on it is equidistant from the endpoints. To find its equation, combine the midpoint with a perpendicular slope."}, {"topic": "geo-centers", "section": 1, "front": "Incenter: equal distance to sides — explain it in your own words.", "back": "The incenter is the common intersection of the triangle’s three internal angle bisectors. It is equidistant from the sides and is the center of the inscribed circle. Distance to a side is measured perpendicularly."}, {"topic": "geo-centers", "section": 2, "front": "Circumcenter: equal distance to corners — explain it in your own words.", "back": "The circumcenter is the intersection of the side perpendicular bisectors. It is equidistant from the three vertices and is the center of the circumscribed circle. It can lie outside the triangle."}, {"topic": "geo-angle-strategy", "section": 0, "front": "Start with one certain relationship — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Identify the smallest useful relationship: linear pair, vertical angles, triangle sum, full turn, bisector, or parallel-line angle pair. Mark known values before solving."}, {"topic": "geo-angle-strategy", "section": 1, "front": "Name the unknown and solve — explain it in your own words.", "back": "For triangle angles x, 2x, and 3x, write x + 2x + 3x = 180. Then x = 30°, giving angles 30°, 60°, and 90°."}, {"topic": "geo-angle-strategy", "section": 2, "front": "Add a helpful line when it unlocks a clue — explain it in your own words.", "back": "An auxiliary line is a line you add to a diagram to make useful relationships visible. A parallel through a vertex can reveal alternate interior or corresponding angles. Explain the reason for adding it."}, {"topic": "geo-angles", "section": 3, "front": "Copy an angle with a compass — explain it in your own words.", "back": "Draw a new base ray. From the original vertex, draw an arc crossing both angle rays. With the same radius, draw an arc from the new vertex. Transfer the distance between the original two arc intersections onto the new arc, then join the new vertex to that mark."}], "reading": {"geo-radicals": {"easy": {"parts": [["What does a root mean?", "√25 asks: what nonnegative number times itself is 25? It is 5. A root undoes a square."], ["Pull out a perfect square", "Break 72 into 36 × 2. The square root of 36 comes out as 6. So √72 = 6√2."], ["Variables need a small safety check", "√(x²) means the distance from zero, so it is |x|. If x cannot be negative, it is just x."]], "trap": "√(a + b) is not generally √a + √b. A radical symbol alone gives the nonnegative root.", "example": "An exact diagonal length such as 6√2 keeps all the precision; 8.49 is only a rounded estimate.", "desc": "√25 asks: what nonnegative number times itself is 25? It is 5. A root undoes a square."}, "medium": {"parts": [["What does a root mean?", "A square root reverses squaring. √a means the principal, nonnegative square root. √25 = 5, while solving x² = 25 gives x = ±5."], ["Pull out a perfect square", "To simplify √72, find a perfect-square factor: √(36 × 2) = 6√2. Keep factoring until no perfect square greater than 1 remains inside."], ["Variables need a small safety check", "√(x²) = |x|, not always x. For example, if x = −3, the root is 3. If a problem states x ≥ 0, then √(x²) = x."]], "trap": "√(a + b) is not generally √a + √b. A radical symbol alone gives the nonnegative root.", "example": "An exact diagonal length such as 6√2 keeps all the precision; 8.49 is only a rounded estimate.", "desc": "A square root reverses squaring. √a means the principal, nonnegative square root. √25 = 5, while solving x² = 25 gives x = ±5."}, "expert": {"parts": [["What does a root mean?", "For a ≥ 0, √a is the unique nonnegative real number whose square is a. A rational number is a ratio of integers; √2 is irrational, but √9 = 3 is rational."], ["Pull out a perfect square", "For nonnegative a and b, √(ab) = √a √b. Extract the greatest perfect-square factor: √72 = √(36·2) = 6√2. This is an exact value; a rounded decimal is an approximation."], ["Variables need a small safety check", "Because the principal root is nonnegative, √(x²) = |x| for real x. For x ≥ 0, √(18x²) = 3x√2; without that assumption it is 3|x|√2."]], "trap": "√(a + b) is not generally √a + √b. A radical symbol alone gives the nonnegative root.", "example": "An exact diagonal length such as 6√2 keeps all the precision; 8.49 is only a rounded estimate.", "desc": "For a ≥ 0, √a is the unique nonnegative real number whose square is a. A rational number is a ratio of integers; √2 is irrational, but √9 = 3 is rational."}}, "geo-radical-operations": {"easy": {"parts": [["Only matching roots combine", "3√2 + 4√2 = 7√2. The √2 is like a label. A √3 has a different label."], ["Multiply every needed pair", "√3 × √12 = √36 = 6. With brackets, multiply each term, just as you do with numbers."], ["Clear a root from the bottom", "For 1/√2, multiply top and bottom by √2. You get √2/2. You changed the look, not the value."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "Conjugates are partners with the same terms and the opposite middle sign. Their product removes the radical cross terms.", "desc": "3√2 + 4√2 = 7√2. The √2 is like a label. A √3 has a different label."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Only matching roots combine", "Combine coefficients only when the simplified radicands match. √8 + √18 = 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2."], ["Multiply every needed pair", "Multiply coefficients and radicands, then simplify. Use distribution: √2(3 + √2) = 3√2 + 2. For two binomials, multiply all four pairs."], ["Clear a root from the bottom", "Rationalize a monomial denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by a suitable radical. For a binomial use its conjugate: (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "Conjugates are partners with the same terms and the opposite middle sign. Their product removes the radical cross terms.", "desc": "Combine coefficients only when the simplified radicands match. √8 + √18 = 2√2 + 3√2 = 5√2."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Only matching roots combine", "Like radicals have the same index and radicand after simplification. Add or subtract their coefficients. Simplification must precede deciding whether radicals are like."], ["Multiply every needed pair", "For real square roots with nonnegative radicands, multiply using √a√b = √(ab). Distribution gives (√3 + 2)(√3 − 2) = 3 − 4 = −1."], ["Clear a root from the bottom", "Multiplying by a nonzero expression divided by itself preserves a fraction. For 1/(√5 + 1), use (√5 − 1)/(√5 − 1), giving (√5 − 1)/4. The original denominator must be nonzero."]], "trap": "Adding roots is different from multiplying roots. √2 + √3 does not become √5.", "example": "Conjugates are partners with the same terms and the opposite middle sign. Their product removes the radical cross terms.", "desc": "Like radicals have the same index and radicand after simplification. Add or subtract their coefficients. Simplification must precede deciding whether radicals are like."}}, "geo-right-triangles": {"easy": {"parts": [["Find the side across from the square corner", "The longest side is across from the 90° corner. Call it c. Then a² + b² = c²."], ["Add to find the long side; subtract for a leg", "Legs 6 and 8 give c² = 36 + 64 = 100. So c = 10. If c is given, subtract the other leg’s square."], ["Coordinates hide a right triangle", "From (1,2) to (4,6), go 3 across and 4 up. The straight distance is √(3² + 4²) = 5."]], "trap": "Never use a² + b² = c² on a triangle unless it is a right triangle. c must be the hypotenuse.", "example": "A square tile with side 5 has diagonal 5√2, not 10: the diagonal cuts a right triangle across the tile.", "desc": "The longest side is across from the 90° corner. Call it c. Then a² + b² = c²."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Find the side across from the square corner", "The Pythagorean theorem applies to right triangles: a² + b² = c². The hypotenuse c is opposite the right angle; a and b are the legs."], ["Add to find the long side; subtract for a leg", "With legs 6 and 8, c = √(36 + 64) = 10. With hypotenuse 10 and one leg 6, the other is √(100 − 36) = 8. Lengths are positive."], ["Coordinates hide a right triangle", "Horizontal and vertical changes form the legs of a right triangle. Distance = √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²). Keep an exact radical unless rounding is requested."]], "trap": "Never use a² + b² = c² on a triangle unless it is a right triangle. c must be the hypotenuse.", "example": "A square tile with side 5 has diagonal 5√2, not 10: the diagonal cuts a right triangle across the tile.", "desc": "The Pythagorean theorem applies to right triangles: a² + b² = c². The hypotenuse c is opposite the right angle; a and b are the legs."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Find the side across from the square corner", "In a Euclidean right triangle, the sum of the squares of the leg lengths equals the square of the hypotenuse. Identify c from the right-angle mark, not the drawing’s orientation."], ["Add to find the long side; subtract for a leg", "Solve for the required squared length before taking the principal root. Reject algebraic roots that produce nonpositive side lengths. For a square of side s, diagonal d = s√2 for s > 0."], ["Coordinates hide a right triangle", "The distance formula follows from the Pythagorean theorem using perpendicular coordinate changes. Reversing both endpoint orders leaves the squared differences unchanged."]], "trap": "Never use a² + b² = c² on a triangle unless it is a right triangle. c must be the hypotenuse.", "example": "A square tile with side 5 has diagonal 5√2, not 10: the diagonal cuts a right triangle across the tile.", "desc": "In a Euclidean right triangle, the sum of the squares of the leg lengths equals the square of the hypotenuse. Identify c from the right-angle mark, not the drawing’s orientation."}}, "geo-factoring": {"easy": {"parts": [["Give the equation a zero side", "Move everything to one side so the other side is zero. Then look for multiplication hiding in the expression."], ["A zero product gives choices", "If two things multiply to zero, at least one must be zero. For (x − 2)(x + 3) = 0, x is 2 or −3."], ["Check the original equation", "Put each answer back into the starting equation. Do both sides match? Then it works."]], "trap": "You cannot set each factor equal to zero until the product itself equals zero.", "example": "A rectangle area equation can become a quadratic. Keep only solutions that give positive physical dimensions.", "desc": "Move everything to one side so the other side is zero. Then look for multiplication hiding in the expression."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Give the equation a zero side", "Write the quadratic in standard form ax² + bx + c = 0, with a ≠ 0. Check for a greatest common factor before factoring further."], ["A zero product gives choices", "The zero-product property says AB = 0 implies A = 0 or B = 0. So x² + x − 6 = (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0 has solutions −3 and 2."], ["Check the original equation", "Substitute each candidate solution into the original equation. Do not lose a solution by dividing by x: x(x − 4) = 0 has both x = 0 and x = 4."]], "trap": "You cannot set each factor equal to zero until the product itself equals zero.", "example": "A rectangle area equation can become a quadratic. Keep only solutions that give positive physical dimensions.", "desc": "Write the quadratic in standard form ax² + bx + c = 0, with a ≠ 0. Check for a greatest common factor before factoring further."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Give the equation a zero side", "Standard form makes coefficients and factor structure visible. An equivalent equation may be obtained by adding, subtracting, or dividing both sides by a nonzero constant."], ["A zero product gives choices", "After factoring completely, set each variable factor equal to zero and solve. The zero-product property applies to products equal to zero, not sums or products equal to another number."], ["Check the original equation", "Verification checks the original relationship and any restrictions from a context. A quadratic can have two, one, or no real roots; repeated factors yield a repeated root."]], "trap": "You cannot set each factor equal to zero until the product itself equals zero.", "example": "A rectangle area equation can become a quadratic. Keep only solutions that give positive physical dimensions.", "desc": "Standard form makes coefficients and factor structure visible. An equivalent equation may be obtained by adding, subtracting, or dividing both sides by a nonzero constant."}}, "geo-formula": {"easy": {"parts": [["Read a, b, and c with their signs", "In 2x² − 3x − 5 = 0, a = 2, b = −3, c = −5. Keep the minus signs attached."], ["The plus-or-minus makes two paths", "Try the plus sign once and the minus sign once. Both use the whole bottom number, 2a."], ["The number under the root is a clue", "b² − 4ac tells how many real answers there are. Positive: two. Zero: one repeated. Negative: none."]], "trap": "The denominator is 2a, not just 2. Squaring a negative b requires parentheses.", "example": "The graph crosses the x-axis twice, touches it once, or misses it according to the discriminant.", "desc": "In 2x² − 3x − 5 = 0, a = 2, b = −3, c = −5. Keep the minus signs attached."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Read a, b, and c with their signs", "First write ax² + bx + c = 0. The quadratic formula is x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a). Use parentheses when substituting negatives."], ["The plus-or-minus makes two paths", "For x² − 2x − 3 = 0, x = (2 ± √16)/2 = (2 ± 4)/2, giving 3 and −1. The entire numerator is divided by 2a."], ["The number under the root is a clue", "The discriminant D = b² − 4ac determines real-root count. D > 0 gives two distinct real roots; D = 0 gives one repeated real root; D < 0 gives no real roots."]], "trap": "The denominator is 2a, not just 2. Squaring a negative b requires parentheses.", "example": "The graph crosses the x-axis twice, touches it once, or misses it according to the discriminant.", "desc": "First write ax² + bx + c = 0. The quadratic formula is x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a). Use parentheses when substituting negatives."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Read a, b, and c with their signs", "For a ≠ 0, the formula solves any quadratic over the complex numbers; here we focus on real roots. Coefficients must be identified from standard form, including their signs."], ["The plus-or-minus makes two paths", "Evaluate the discriminant first, simplify its square root, then reduce the full fraction. If the discriminant is zero, the two formula branches coincide."], ["The number under the root is a clue", "For real coefficients, D classifies the real roots. With integer coefficients, a positive perfect-square discriminant produces rational roots; a positive nonsquare discriminant produces irrational roots."]], "trap": "The denominator is 2a, not just 2. Squaring a negative b requires parentheses.", "example": "The graph crosses the x-axis twice, touches it once, or misses it according to the discriminant.", "desc": "For a ≠ 0, the formula solves any quadratic over the complex numbers; here we focus on real roots. Coefficients must be identified from standard form, including their signs."}}, "geo-complete-square": {"easy": {"parts": [["Make the x² coefficient one", "If a number sits before x², divide every term by it first. Move the plain number to the other side."], ["Half, then square, then both sides", "For x² + 6x, half of 6 is 3. Square 3 to get 9. Add 9 to BOTH sides."], ["Finish with both square-root choices", "x² + 6x = 7 becomes (x + 3)² = 16. So x + 3 is 4 or −4. x is 1 or −7."]], "trap": "Adding to just one side changes the equation. Taking a square root without ± can lose a solution.", "example": "Completing a square turns an awkward quadratic into one squared expression you already know how to undo.", "desc": "If a number sits before x², divide every term by it first. Move the plain number to the other side."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Make the x² coefficient one", "To complete the square, divide by the leading coefficient if needed, then move the constant. Start with x² + bx = k."], ["Half, then square, then both sides", "Add (b/2)² to both sides of x² + bx = k. Then x² + bx + (b/2)² = (x + b/2)². Keeping both sides balanced preserves equality."], ["Finish with both square-root choices", "After forming a square, use ± when taking square roots to solve the equation. (x + 3)² = 16 gives x = −3 ± 4, or 1 and −7."]], "trap": "Adding to just one side changes the equation. Taking a square root without ± can lose a solution.", "example": "Completing a square turns an awkward quadratic into one squared expression you already know how to undo.", "desc": "To complete the square, divide by the leading coefficient if needed, then move the constant. Start with x² + bx = k."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Make the x² coefficient one", "For ax² + bx + c = 0 with a ≠ 0, dividing by a normalizes the quadratic term. Isolate the variable terms before forming a perfect-square trinomial."], ["Half, then square, then both sides", "The identity (x + h)² = x² + 2hx + h² explains the method: h = b/2 after normalization. Fractional values of h are allowed."], ["Finish with both square-root choices", "Solve the resulting equation using the square-root property and isolate x. The principal radical √16 is 4, but an equation u² = 16 has u = ±4."]], "trap": "Adding to just one side changes the equation. Taking a square root without ± can lose a solution.", "example": "Completing a square turns an awkward quadratic into one squared expression you already know how to undo.", "desc": "For ax² + bx + c = 0 with a ≠ 0, dividing by a normalizes the quadratic term. Isolate the variable terms before forming a perfect-square trinomial."}}, "geo-systems": {"easy": {"parts": [["One answer must fit both rules", "A system is two rules at once. Its answer must make BOTH equations true. On a graph, look where they meet."], ["Substitute or eliminate", "If y = x + 1, use x + 1 wherever you see y in the other equation. Or add equations so one letter cancels."], ["Translate a rectangle story", "A rectangle with length L and width W has area LW and perimeter 2L + 2W. These give two rules for the same shape."]], "trap": "An x-value alone is not a full ordered-pair solution. Check both coordinates in both original equations.", "example": "For y = x² and y = x + 2, intersections satisfy x² = x + 2, giving (−1,1) and (2,4).", "desc": "A system is two rules at once. Its answer must make BOTH equations true. On a graph, look where they meet."}, "medium": {"parts": [["One answer must fit both rules", "A solution to a two-variable system is an ordered pair (x,y) satisfying every equation. Graphically it is an intersection point."], ["Substitute or eliminate", "Substitution replaces a variable with an equal expression. Elimination multiplies equations as needed and adds or subtracts them to cancel a variable. Solve, then find the other coordinate."], ["Translate a rectangle story", "For area 24 and perimeter 20: LW = 24 and L + W = 10. Substitute W = 10 − L to get L(10 − L) = 24. The dimensions are 4 and 6."]], "trap": "An x-value alone is not a full ordered-pair solution. Check both coordinates in both original equations.", "example": "For y = x² and y = x + 2, intersections satisfy x² = x + 2, giving (−1,1) and (2,4).", "desc": "A solution to a two-variable system is an ordered pair (x,y) satisfying every equation. Graphically it is an intersection point."}, "expert": {"parts": [["One answer must fit both rules", "The solution set is the intersection of the equations’ solution sets. A linear–quadratic system can have zero, one, or two real intersection points."], ["Substitute or eliminate", "Equivalent substitution and linear combinations reduce the number of unknowns. In a linear–quadratic system, substitution often produces a quadratic; each real root must be paired with its matching y-value."], ["Translate a rectangle story", "Use consistent units and positive dimensions. The roots 4 and 6 of L² − 10L + 24 = 0 describe the same rectangle when length and width labels are exchanged."]], "trap": "An x-value alone is not a full ordered-pair solution. Check both coordinates in both original equations.", "example": "For y = x² and y = x + 2, intersections satisfy x² = x + 2, giving (−1,1) and (2,4).", "desc": "The solution set is the intersection of the equations’ solution sets. A linear–quadratic system can have zero, one, or two real intersection points."}}, "geo-points": {"easy": {"parts": [["Know what stops and what keeps going", "A point is a location. A segment stops at two ends. A ray starts at one end and keeps going. A line keeps going both ways."], ["Read coordinates in order", "For (−2,3), go left 2, then up 3. Always x first, y second."], ["Pieces make the whole", "If B is between A and C, add AB and BC to get AC. If AC is 15 and AB is 6, BC is 9."]], "trap": "Ray AB and ray BA generally point in different directions. Do not add pieces unless the diagram or text establishes their positions.", "example": "A trip from A to C through a point B on the segment has two lengths that add to the full distance.", "desc": "A point is a location. A segment stops at two ends. A ray starts at one end and keeps going. A line keeps going both ways."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Know what stops and what keeps going", "A point has no dimensions. A line extends infinitely in both directions. A segment has two endpoints. A ray has one endpoint and extends infinitely in one direction."], ["Read coordinates in order", "In (x,y), x gives horizontal position and y vertical position. Quadrants I, II, III, IV have signs (+,+), (−,+), (−,−), (+,−). Points on axes are not in quadrants."], ["Pieces make the whole", "Segment addition: if B lies between A and C, AB + BC = AC. For AB = 2x, BC = x + 3, AC = 18, solve 3x + 3 = 18, so x = 5."]], "trap": "Ray AB and ray BA generally point in different directions. Do not add pieces unless the diagram or text establishes their positions.", "example": "A trip from A to C through a point B on the segment has two lengths that add to the full distance.", "desc": "A point has no dimensions. A line extends infinitely in both directions. A segment has two endpoints. A ray has one endpoint and extends infinitely in one direction."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Know what stops and what keeps going", "Points identify positions; lines and rays have infinite extent, while segments have finite length. Name a ray with its endpoint first: ray AB begins at A and passes through B."], ["Read coordinates in order", "The Cartesian plane uses perpendicular x- and y-axes. Ordered pairs locate points uniquely; reversing coordinates generally gives a different point."], ["Pieces make the whole", "Between-ness is required for this segment-addition equation. Segment notation denotes the geometric set; AB without a segment bar commonly denotes its length. Copy a length with a compass by transferring the endpoint distance."]], "trap": "Ray AB and ray BA generally point in different directions. Do not add pieces unless the diagram or text establishes their positions.", "example": "A trip from A to C through a point B on the segment has two lengths that add to the full distance.", "desc": "Points identify positions; lines and rays have infinite extent, while segments have finite length. Name a ray with its endpoint first: ray AB begins at A and passes through B."}}, "geo-midpoints": {"easy": {"parts": [["A midpoint makes equal halves", "A midpoint is the exact middle of a segment. The two little lengths are equal."], ["Average x, then average y", "Between (0,2) and (6,8), the middle x is 3 and the middle y is 5. The midpoint is (3,5)."], ["Construct the middle with equal arcs", "Open a compass more than halfway across the segment. Draw equal-size arcs from both ends. Join their two crossing points."]], "trap": "A segment bisector alone guarantees equal halves, not a 90° angle.", "example": "If one half is 3x + 1 and the other is x + 9, set them equal: x = 4, so each half is 13.", "desc": "A midpoint is the exact middle of a segment. The two little lengths are equal."}, "medium": {"parts": [["A midpoint makes equal halves", "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint."], ["Average x, then average y", "M = ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2). For a missing endpoint, double the midpoint coordinates and subtract the known endpoint coordinates."], ["Construct the middle with equal arcs", "Use the same compass radius, greater than half the segment length, from both endpoints. Join the two arc intersections. This line crosses the segment at its midpoint and at 90°."]], "trap": "A segment bisector alone guarantees equal halves, not a 90° angle.", "example": "If one half is 3x + 1 and the other is x + 9, set them equal: x = 4, so each half is 13.", "desc": "If M is the midpoint of AB, AM = MB and AB = 2AM. A segment bisector is a line, ray, or segment passing through that midpoint."}, "expert": {"parts": [["A midpoint makes equal halves", "A midpoint partitions a segment into two congruent segments. A segment bisector need not be perpendicular; passing through the midpoint is the defining condition."], ["Average x, then average y", "The midpoint formula averages coordinate components independently. If M is the midpoint of AB, then B = (2Mₓ − Aₓ, 2Mᵧ − Aᵧ)."], ["Construct the middle with equal arcs", "Equal-radius intersecting circles centered at A and B determine two points equidistant from A and B. Their connecting line is the perpendicular bisector. A radius at or below half the segment length does not give two distinct intersections."]], "trap": "A segment bisector alone guarantees equal halves, not a 90° angle.", "example": "If one half is 3x + 1 and the other is x + 9, set them equal: x = 4, so each half is 13.", "desc": "A midpoint partitions a segment into two congruent segments. A segment bisector need not be perpendicular; passing through the midpoint is the defining condition."}}, "geo-slope": {"easy": {"parts": [["Slope measures steepness and direction", "Slope tells how much y changes when x changes. Go up 2 and right 1: slope is 2."], ["Horizontal is zero; vertical is undefined", "A flat line has no rise, so slope is 0. A vertical line has no run; you cannot divide by 0."], ["Check whether points share a line", "Points are collinear if one straight line goes through them. Compare slopes from the same point."]], "trap": "Zero slope and undefined slope are different. A vertical line is x = a number, not y = a number.", "example": "A ramp rising 2 units across a horizontal run of 5 has slope 2/5.", "desc": "Slope tells how much y changes when x changes. Go up 2 and right 1: slope is 2."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Slope measures steepness and direction", "m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), when x₂ ≠ x₁. Keep endpoint order consistent. Positive slopes rise left to right; negative slopes fall."], ["Horizontal is zero; vertical is undefined", "A horizontal line has equation y = k and slope 0. A vertical line has equation x = k and undefined slope. Remember HOY / VUX."], ["Check whether points share a line", "For distinct points, equal slopes from a shared point show collinearity, provided the slopes exist. If all x-coordinates match, the points lie on one vertical line."]], "trap": "Zero slope and undefined slope are different. A vertical line is x = a number, not y = a number.", "example": "A ramp rising 2 units across a horizontal run of 5 has slope 2/5.", "desc": "m = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁), when x₂ ≠ x₁. Keep endpoint order consistent. Positive slopes rise left to right; negative slopes fall."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Slope measures steepness and direction", "Slope is the ratio of vertical to horizontal coordinate change. Reversing both subtraction orders gives the same ratio; reversing only one changes the sign incorrectly."], ["Horizontal is zero; vertical is undefined", "For horizontal distinct points, Δy = 0 and Δx ≠ 0, giving m = 0. Vertical distinct points have Δx = 0, so their slope is undefined rather than infinite."], ["Check whether points share a line", "Collinear points lie on one line. Handle undefined slopes explicitly: common x-coordinates establish a vertical line, while equality of two defined slopes from a common point establishes nonvertical collinearity."]], "trap": "Zero slope and undefined slope are different. A vertical line is x = a number, not y = a number.", "example": "A ramp rising 2 units across a horizontal run of 5 has slope 2/5.", "desc": "Slope is the ratio of vertical to horizontal coordinate change. Reversing both subtraction orders gives the same ratio; reversing only one changes the sign incorrectly."}}, "geo-line-equations": {"easy": {"parts": [["Use y = mx + b", "m is the slope. b is where the line hits the y-axis. For slope 2 and b = 3, write y = 2x + 3."], ["Use a point to find b", "Slope 3 through (2,7)? Put 2 and 7 into y = 3x + b. Then 7 = 6 + b, so b = 1."], ["Check the finished rule", "Put the given point into your final equation. The two sides should match."]], "trap": "The b is a y-intercept, not whichever y-value appears in the given point.", "example": "Through (1,4) and (3,8), slope is 2 and b is 2, giving y = 2x + 2.", "desc": "m is the slope. b is where the line hits the y-axis. For slope 2 and b = 3, write y = 2x + 3."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Use y = mx + b", "In y = mx + b, m is slope and (0,b) is the y-intercept. This form describes nonvertical lines. A vertical line is x = k."], ["Use a point to find b", "Find m, substitute a given point into y = mx + b, solve for b, then write the equation using variables x and y. With m = 3 through (2,7), y = 3x + 1."], ["Check the finished rule", "For y = 3x + 1, the point (2,7) works because 7 = 3(2) + 1. With two given points, check both; also check that the slope matches."]], "trap": "The b is a y-intercept, not whichever y-value appears in the given point.", "example": "Through (1,4) and (3,8), slope is 2 and b is 2, giving y = 2x + 2.", "desc": "In y = mx + b, m is slope and (0,b) is the y-intercept. This form describes nonvertical lines. A vertical line is x = k."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Use y = mx + b", "Slope-intercept form parameterizes nonvertical lines by slope and y-intercept. Horizontal lines fit with m = 0; vertical lines require a separate equation."], ["Use a point to find b", "Given two points, first compute their slope unless the x-coordinates match. Then solve b = y₁ − mx₁. Equivalent point-slope form is y − y₁ = m(x − x₁)."], ["Check the finished rule", "Verification checks both incidence and direction. A line through two distinct points is unique; a point and a specified slope likewise determine one line."]], "trap": "The b is a y-intercept, not whichever y-value appears in the given point.", "example": "Through (1,4) and (3,8), slope is 2 and b is 2, giving y = 2x + 2.", "desc": "Slope-intercept form parameterizes nonvertical lines by slope and y-intercept. Horizontal lines fit with m = 0; vertical lines require a separate equation."}}, "geo-angles": {"easy": {"parts": [["Name and measure an angle", "Two rays with the same starting point make an angle. In ∠ABC, B is the corner."], ["90, 180, or 360?", "Two angles adding to 90° are complementary. Adding to 180° makes them supplementary. A full turn is 360°."], ["Bisect means two equal angles", "A bisector splits an angle right down the middle. Half of 110° is 55°."], ["Copy an angle with a compass", "Draw a new starting ray. Use equal-size arcs at the old and new corners. Copy the distance between the old arc marks to the new arc, then draw the new ray."]], "trap": "A picture that looks like equal angles is not proof. Use markings, a stated bisector, or a known relationship.", "example": "For adjacent angles 28° and 47°, the whole is 75°. Its bisector would make two 37.5° angles.", "desc": "Two rays with the same starting point make an angle. In ∠ABC, B is the corner."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Name and measure an angle", "An angle is formed by two rays sharing a vertex. The vertex is the middle letter in a three-letter name. Acute: 0° < θ < 90°; right: 90°; obtuse: 90° < θ < 180°; straight: 180°."], ["90, 180, or 360?", "Complementary angles sum to 90°; supplementary angles sum to 180°. A linear pair forms a straight angle, so its angles sum to 180°. Angles around one point sum to 360°."], ["Bisect means two equal angles", "An angle bisector divides an angle into two congruent angles. For halves 3x + 5 and 5x − 15, equate them: x = 10, so each half is 35° and the whole is 70°."], ["Copy an angle with a compass", "Draw a new base ray. From the original vertex, draw an arc crossing both angle rays. With the same radius, draw an arc from the new vertex. Transfer the distance between the original two arc intersections onto the new arc, then join the new vertex to that mark."]], "trap": "A picture that looks like equal angles is not proof. Use markings, a stated bisector, or a known relationship.", "example": "For adjacent angles 28° and 47°, the whole is 75°. Its bisector would make two 37.5° angles.", "desc": "An angle is formed by two rays sharing a vertex. The vertex is the middle letter in a three-letter name. Acute: 0° < θ < 90°; right: 90°; obtuse: 90° < θ < 180°; straight: 180°."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Name and measure an angle", "Angle names place the vertex between points on the two rays. A lone vertex letter is sufficient only when it unambiguously names the angle. Adjacent component measures add to the whole angle."], ["90, 180, or 360?", "Complementary and supplementary relationships concern sums, not necessarily adjacency. Vertical angles are congruent. Adjacent angles forming a straight line are supplementary."], ["Bisect means two equal angles", "A ray bisecting an angle creates equal angle measures, unlike an arbitrary interior ray. For a compass construction, mark equal distances on both rays, draw equal intersecting arcs from those marks, then connect the vertex to their intersection."], ["Copy an angle with a compass", "Preserve the original vertex-to-arc radius in the new construction. Transfer the chord length between the original ray–arc intersections to the new arc. The resulting triangle has three matching side lengths, so the constructed angle is congruent to the original."]], "trap": "A picture that looks like equal angles is not proof. Use markings, a stated bisector, or a known relationship.", "example": "For adjacent angles 28° and 47°, the whole is 75°. Its bisector would make two 37.5° angles.", "desc": "Angle names place the vertex between points on the two rays. A lone vertex letter is sufficient only when it unambiguously names the angle. Adjacent component measures add to the whole angle."}}, "geo-parallel": {"easy": {"parts": [["A transversal crosses two lines", "A transversal is a line cutting across other lines. Look at where each angle sits, not how big it looks."], ["Spot matching or adding pairs", "Corresponding angles sit in matching corners. Alternate interior angles are inside the lines on opposite sides of the crossing line."], ["Turn the relationship into an equation", "If matching angles are 2x + 10 and 70, set 2x + 10 = 70. If they add to 180, use a sum instead."]], "trap": "The special equal-angle rules require parallel lines. A transversal alone is not enough.", "example": "If one angle in the parallel-line model is 65°, all the acute angles are 65° and all the obtuse angles are 115°.", "desc": "A transversal is a line cutting across other lines. Look at where each angle sits, not how big it looks."}, "medium": {"parts": [["A transversal crosses two lines", "For two distinct coplanar parallel lines cut by a transversal, corresponding, alternate interior, and alternate exterior angles are congruent. Same-side interior angles are supplementary."], ["Spot matching or adding pairs", "Corresponding angles occupy the same relative position at each intersection. Alternate interior pairs are between the lines on opposite sides of the transversal; alternate exterior pairs are outside on opposite sides."], ["Turn the relationship into an equation", "For parallel lines, corresponding angles 2x + 10 and 70 yield x = 30. Same-side interior angles 2x and 3x yield 5x = 180, so x = 36."]], "trap": "The special equal-angle rules require parallel lines. A transversal alone is not enough.", "example": "If one angle in the parallel-line model is 65°, all the acute angles are 65° and all the obtuse angles are 115°.", "desc": "For two distinct coplanar parallel lines cut by a transversal, corresponding, alternate interior, and alternate exterior angles are congruent. Same-side interior angles are supplementary."}, "expert": {"parts": [["A transversal crosses two lines", "Parallel lines in a plane do not intersect. The packet also treats coincident lines as having the same direction; here the usual distinct-line parallel-angle theorems use two separate parallel lines."], ["Spot matching or adding pairs", "Use position to identify the relationship before writing an equation. Parallelism establishes congruence for corresponding and alternate pairs; same-side interior measures sum to 180°."], ["Turn the relationship into an equation", "The relationship determines equality versus supplementation. Check the resulting angle measures against both the given expressions and the required total. Copying a corresponding angle through an external point constructs a parallel line."]], "trap": "The special equal-angle rules require parallel lines. A transversal alone is not enough.", "example": "If one angle in the parallel-line model is 65°, all the acute angles are 65° and all the obtuse angles are 115°.", "desc": "Parallel lines in a plane do not intersect. The packet also treats coincident lines as having the same direction; here the usual distinct-line parallel-angle theorems use two separate parallel lines."}}, "geo-perpendicular": {"easy": {"parts": [["Parallel lines share direction", "Parallel nonvertical lines have the same slope. Different heights can still mean the same steepness."], ["Perpendicular lines make 90°", "For a nonzero slope, flip its fraction and change its sign. A slope of 2/3 pairs with −3/2."], ["Find the new line through a point", "Choose the needed slope first. Then use your point to find b in y = mx + b."]], "trap": "Changing only the sign is not enough: the perpendicular slope to 2 is −1/2, not −2.", "example": "A line parallel to y = 3x − 4 through (2,1) is y = 3x − 5.", "desc": "Parallel nonvertical lines have the same slope. Different heights can still mean the same steepness."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Parallel lines share direction", "Distinct nonvertical parallel lines have equal slopes and different intercepts. Distinct vertical lines are parallel to each other."], ["Perpendicular lines make 90°", "Nonvertical perpendicular lines have slopes whose product is −1. Use the negative reciprocal. Horizontal and vertical lines are perpendicular to each other."], ["Find the new line through a point", "A line perpendicular to y = 2x + 1 through (0,3) has slope −1/2 and intercept 3: y = −x/2 + 3. For a vertical answer, use x = k."]], "trap": "Changing only the sign is not enough: the perpendicular slope to 2 is −1/2, not −2.", "example": "A line parallel to y = 3x − 4 through (2,1) is y = 3x − 5.", "desc": "Distinct nonvertical parallel lines have equal slopes and different intercepts. Distinct vertical lines are parallel to each other."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Parallel lines share direction", "Equal finite slopes give parallel or coincident lines. Distinct intercepts distinguish separate lines. Vertical parallel lines have undefined slopes, handled separately."], ["Perpendicular lines make 90°", "For nonzero finite m, a perpendicular slope is −1/m. The zero-slope case has a vertical perpendicular and cannot be handled by dividing by zero."], ["Find the new line through a point", "To construct a perpendicular through a point, identify two equal-distance intersections on the original line, then use equal arcs from those intersections to locate a second point on the perpendicular."]], "trap": "Changing only the sign is not enough: the perpendicular slope to 2 is −1/2, not −2.", "example": "A line parallel to y = 3x − 4 through (2,1) is y = 3x − 5.", "desc": "Equal finite slopes give parallel or coincident lines. Distinct intercepts distinguish separate lines. Vertical parallel lines have undefined slopes, handled separately."}}, "geo-centers": {"easy": {"parts": [["A perpendicular bisector does two jobs", "It cuts a segment in half AND makes a square corner. Any point on it is equally far from the segment’s two ends."], ["Incenter: equal distance to sides", "A triangle’s angle bisectors meet at the incenter. It is inside the triangle. A circle centered there can touch all three sides."], ["Circumcenter: equal distance to corners", "A triangle’s side perpendicular bisectors meet at the circumcenter. A circle centered there passes through all three corners."]], "trap": "Incenter uses angle bisectors and distances to sides. Circumcenter uses perpendicular bisectors and distances to vertices.", "example": "For a segment from (0,0) to (4,0), the perpendicular bisector is x = 2; every point on it is equally far from both endpoints.", "desc": "It cuts a segment in half AND makes a square corner. Any point on it is equally far from the segment’s two ends."}, "medium": {"parts": [["A perpendicular bisector does two jobs", "A perpendicular bisector passes through a segment’s midpoint at 90°. Every point on it is equidistant from the endpoints. To find its equation, combine the midpoint with a perpendicular slope."], ["Incenter: equal distance to sides", "The incenter is the common intersection of the triangle’s three internal angle bisectors. It is equidistant from the sides and is the center of the inscribed circle. Distance to a side is measured perpendicularly."], ["Circumcenter: equal distance to corners", "The circumcenter is the intersection of the side perpendicular bisectors. It is equidistant from the three vertices and is the center of the circumscribed circle. It can lie outside the triangle."]], "trap": "Incenter uses angle bisectors and distances to sides. Circumcenter uses perpendicular bisectors and distances to vertices.", "example": "For a segment from (0,0) to (4,0), the perpendicular bisector is x = 2; every point on it is equally far from both endpoints.", "desc": "A perpendicular bisector passes through a segment’s midpoint at 90°. Every point on it is equidistant from the endpoints. To find its equation, combine the midpoint with a perpendicular slope."}, "expert": {"parts": [["A perpendicular bisector does two jobs", "The perpendicular bisector is the locus of points equidistant from the two endpoints. Coordinate construction uses the midpoint and negative reciprocal slope, with horizontal/vertical exceptions."], ["Incenter: equal distance to sides", "Two internal angle bisectors suffice to locate the incenter; the third passes through the same point. Use a perpendicular radius to a side to set the incircle radius, not the distance to a vertex."], ["Circumcenter: equal distance to corners", "The circumcenter is inside an acute triangle, at the hypotenuse midpoint of a right triangle, and outside an obtuse triangle. Construct two side perpendicular bisectors, then use the distance to any vertex as the circumradius."]], "trap": "Incenter uses angle bisectors and distances to sides. Circumcenter uses perpendicular bisectors and distances to vertices.", "example": "For a segment from (0,0) to (4,0), the perpendicular bisector is x = 2; every point on it is equally far from both endpoints.", "desc": "The perpendicular bisector is the locus of points equidistant from the two endpoints. Coordinate construction uses the midpoint and negative reciprocal slope, with horizontal/vertical exceptions."}}, "geo-angle-strategy": {"easy": {"parts": [["Start with one certain relationship", "Mark what you know. A straight line totals 180°. A triangle totals 180°. A full turn totals 360°."], ["Name the unknown and solve", "If a triangle has 50° and 60°, the last angle is 180 − 50 − 60 = 70°."], ["Add a helpful line when it unlocks a clue", "Sometimes drawing an extra line makes a familiar angle pair appear. Add it for a reason and say what it helps you use."]], "trap": "If the problem asks for an angle written 3x + 5, finding x is only the first step. Substitute to finish.", "example": "If a triangle’s angles are x + 10, 2x, and 80°, then 3x + 90 = 180, so x = 30° and the angles are 40°, 60°, 80°.", "desc": "Mark what you know. A straight line totals 180°. A triangle totals 180°. A full turn totals 360°."}, "medium": {"parts": [["Start with one certain relationship", "Identify the smallest useful relationship: linear pair, vertical angles, triangle sum, full turn, bisector, or parallel-line angle pair. Mark known values before solving."], ["Name the unknown and solve", "For triangle angles x, 2x, and 3x, write x + 2x + 3x = 180. Then x = 30°, giving angles 30°, 60°, and 90°."], ["Add a helpful line when it unlocks a clue", "An auxiliary line is a line you add to a diagram to make useful relationships visible. A parallel through a vertex can reveal alternate interior or corresponding angles. Explain the reason for adding it."]], "trap": "If the problem asks for an angle written 3x + 5, finding x is only the first step. Substitute to finish.", "example": "If a triangle’s angles are x + 10, 2x, and 80°, then 3x + 90 = 180, so x = 30° and the angles are 40°, 60°, 80°.", "desc": "Identify the smallest useful relationship: linear pair, vertical angles, triangle sum, full turn, bisector, or parallel-line angle pair. Mark known values before solving."}, "expert": {"parts": [["Start with one certain relationship", "Use the givens and established theorems to justify each equation. Diagram appearance alone does not establish parallelism, perpendicularity, or congruence."], ["Name the unknown and solve", "Translate the angle relationship into an algebraic equation, solve it, then evaluate every requested angle expression. An algebraic variable is not always itself the angle measure."], ["Add a helpful line when it unlocks a clue", "An auxiliary construction must have a specified property, such as passing through a point parallel to a given line. Use that property to justify subsequent equalities, then check all angle sums and constraints."]], "trap": "If the problem asks for an angle written 3x + 5, finding x is only the first step. Substitute to finish.", "example": "If a triangle’s angles are x + 10, 2x, and 80°, then 3x + 90 = 180, so x = 30° and the angles are 40°, 60°, 80°.", "desc": "Use the givens and established theorems to justify each equation. Diagram appearance alone does not establish parallelism, perpendicularity, or congruence."}}}};
TOPICS.push(...GEOMETRY.topics);QUESTIONS.push(...GEOMETRY.questions);CARDS.push(...GEOMETRY.cards);
